WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.360
Verbose tool output has three practical fixes, and this lesson covers all three.

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.560
For this one, we'll look at field level filtering, model-driven summarization, and

3
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.800
reference indirection. When each one is the right call, the framework for

4
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.239
choosing between them, and the anti-pattern that can make trimming worse

5
00:00:19.239 --> 00:00:24.680
than not trimming at all. Let's get into it. There is more than one way to cut a

6
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:29.559
payload down, and picking the wrong one costs you either money or reliability.

7
00:00:29.559 --> 00:00:35.040
Field level filtering is deterministic. You declare a fixed allow list of fields

8
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.799
and drop everything else at the tool wrapper, which is the cheapest option

9
00:00:38.799 --> 00:00:44.080
when the schema is stable. Model-driven summarization routes the raw output

10
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.639
through a small model that compresses it into what the main agent actually needs.

11
00:00:48.639 --> 00:00:54.159
It's more flexible, but it adds one model call. Reference indirection stores the

12
00:00:54.159 --> 00:00:59.200
full payload externally, and hands the agent only a compact identifier it can

13
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.360
use to fetch detail if and when the task requires it. Each has a distinct

14
00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:10.760
cost profile, a distinct latency profile, and a distinct best-use case. Field level

15
00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:14.559
filtering is the most straightforward trim you can apply, and it should be your

16
00:01:14.559 --> 00:01:19.160
default when conditions allow. You configure the tool wrapper with an allow

17
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:24.080
list of the specific fields your agent needs for its task. When the tool returns

18
00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.400
its response, the wrapper runs a deterministic JSON path extraction and

19
00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:34.720
returns only those allow listed fields. No model is involved, no extra latency is

20
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.720
added, and the reduction is guaranteed every time. The key requirement is that

21
00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:44.720
the tool schema has to be stable and versioned. If fields move, get renamed, or

22
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.080
get nested differently across API versions, a fixed allow list will

23
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.599
silently miss them, and your agent will start failing in ways that are hard to

24
00:01:53.599 --> 00:01:58.400
trace. For predictable versioned APIs, filtering is the best and most

25
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.760
cost-effective starting point. The choice between filtering and

26
00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:07.519
summarization comes down almost entirely to schema stability. When the tool's

27
00:02:07.519 --> 00:02:13.160
response structure is predictable and versioned, filtering works perfectly. You

28
00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:17.600
declare the fields once, the wrapper runs the same extraction every time, and

29
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:23.240
there's nothing to adapt to. But when the schema changes across providers, across

30
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:29.279
API versions, or when the tool produces dynamic output structures that vary by

31
00:02:29.279 --> 00:02:34.759
call, a fixed field list breaks down. A field you relied on yesterday may be

32
00:02:34.759 --> 00:02:39.720
renamed, moved to a nested object, or combined with another field today. In

33
00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:45.600
that case, routing the raw output through a small summarization model gives you

34
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.800
the adaptability that a static allow list simply can't provide. The model

35
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.000
reads whatever structure arrives and returns what your agent needs. Model

36
00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.679
driven summarization adds one lightweight step between the tool call

37
00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:04.240
and the main agent. You route the raw payload through a small, inexpensive

38
00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:08.839
model, along with a task scoped prompt that describes what the main agent is

39
00:03:08.839 --> 00:03:13.600
trying to accomplish. That small model returns a compact, relevant summary

40
00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:19.000
focused on the task at hand. The summary, not the raw payload, enters the main

41
00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.839
agent's context. Yes, you're adding one model call and a small amount of

42
00:03:23.839 --> 00:03:29.000
latency, but the arithmetic usually works out strongly in your favor. If the raw

43
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:36.679
payload is 2,000 tokens and the summary is 150, you've removed 1,850 tokens of

44
00:03:36.679 --> 00:03:42.199
context pressure in exchange for one small model call. For schemas that change

45
00:03:42.199 --> 00:03:47.720
frequently, that trade is worth it every time. Here's a simple decision framework

46
00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:51.679
for choosing between the three strategies. If the schema is stable and

47
00:03:51.679 --> 00:03:56.360
versioned, start with field level filtering. It's free to run, fully

48
00:03:56.360 --> 00:04:01.279
deterministic, and requires no model involvement at all. If the schema is

49
00:04:01.279 --> 00:04:05.520
dynamic or varies across providers or versions, use model driven

50
00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.559
summarization. The small model call adapts to any structure the tool returns

51
00:04:10.559 --> 00:04:15.360
and extracts what you need regardless. If the payload is very large and the

52
00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.519
agent may not need most of it to complete its current task, reach for

53
00:04:19.519 --> 00:04:24.399
reference in direction. Store the full response in an external cache, hand the

54
00:04:24.399 --> 00:04:29.600
agent a compact identifier, and let it fetch only if the task actually requires

55
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:35.359
it. In practice, many agent tasks complete without that fetch ever happening, which

56
00:04:35.359 --> 00:04:39.279
means the payload never touches the context window at all.

57
00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:43.160
The Trim Audit Log deserves its own attention because it's easy to skip

58
00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:46.760
during initial development and genuinely painful to add after a

59
00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:51.799
production incident. The rule is simple. Every time you trim layer, the wrapper

60
00:04:51.799 --> 00:04:55.640
component sitting between the raw tool response and the agent's context

61
00:04:55.640 --> 00:05:00.359
discards a field. It should write a log entry recording the field name, the call

62
00:05:00.359 --> 00:05:05.119
time stamp, and what was kept. When a downstream step fails or produces a

63
00:05:05.119 --> 00:05:10.040
wrong answer, your first check is that log. Overtrimming, dropping a field the

64
00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:14.839
agent later needs, forces an expensive recall, and the log is how you catch it

65
00:05:14.839 --> 00:05:19.279
without guessing. Reviewing those logs over time also tells you when your

66
00:05:19.279 --> 00:05:24.160
allow list has grown too narrow for a workflow that has changed around it. The

67
00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:27.920
right trim strategy depends on how stable your schema is, how large the

68
00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:33.160
payloads are, and how likely the agent is to need the full content. Filter when you

69
00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:38.359
can, summarize when the structure varies, use indirection for the largest payloads,

70
00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.279
and log every field you drop so an overtrim is something you can diagnose

71
00:05:43.279 --> 00:05:48.839
rather than guess at later. Noise removed at the wrapper never costs you

72
00:05:48.839 --> 00:05:53.279
downstream, which is the whole benefit here. And that's it for this lesson. I'll

73
00:05:53.279 --> 00:05:56.239
see you in the next one.

